    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d445">

                <pb facs="0762" n="748" sameAs="frus:frus1949v05/pg748"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24/10–1949</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by the Under Secretary of
                        State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Webb</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1949-10-19">October 19, 1949</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <table>
                        <row role="label">
                            <cell>Participants:</cell>
                            <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. Vladimir I. Bazykin, Counselor,
                                Soviet Embassy</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. Webb, Under Secretary</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. Thompson, Deputy Assistant
                                Secretary for European Affairs</cell>
                        </row>
                    </table>
                </p>
                <p>Mr. Bazykin stated that during the piloting of icebreakers “North Wind”, “West
                    Wind” and “South Wind” through the Arctic regions in the westward direction in
                    order to transfer them to the United States authorities in Bremerhaven, the said
                    icebreakers, because of an exceptionally heavy ice situation, created in October
                    of this year in the Eastern region of the Arctic, have been gripped in solid
                    ice. Because of this reason the transfer of the icebreakers in October–November
                    of 1949 to the United States authorities in the port of Bremerhaven (Germany),
                    as it was provided for by the agreement of September.27, cannot be realized.</p>
                <p>He therefore suggested that the agreement be modified to propose for the delivery
                    of the icebreakers in northern Japanese ports at the beginning of December. I
                    said I would have to get in touch with our naval authorities and would let him
                    know.</p>
                <p>Mr. Thompson pointed out that our naval authorities had found <pb facs="0763"
                        n="749" xml:id="pg749"/> the delivery of naval ships to northern Japanese
                    ports impracticable.</p>
                <p>Mr. Bazykin said he had no instructions other than to propose delivery to
                    northern Japanese ports.</p>
            </div>
